 xanthian@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG (Kent Paul Dolan) writes:
[a reprise and more detailed version of the "checkerboard" algorithm for
 rings of conflict]

 sam@math.ucla.edu (Sam Needham) writes:

> I like this with reservations. A monster on the diagonal from the
> player will be on the same colour space every turn and so be 50/50
> naughty and nice. However a monster walking towards the player along a
> rectangular direction, and starting out mean, will stay mean and head
> towards the player until it hits - because it will move one closer,
> change square colour, and stay mean.

Well, but that's OK, because 1) in the interesting situations, the
straight line pathway is going to be blocked by monsters doing their
random or monster attacking move, so a straight approach won't be
possible, and 2) even if the path happens to be free, the incoming
monster takes one whack, and then is in conflict the next turn.

> Secondly, a smart player will be able to predict who's going to attack
> him or her this round and take steps to avoid the most dangerous.

Sure, but that's what Nethack's all about, learning the game's foibles
and then figuring out ways to take advantage of them; I consider this
one a feature, not a bug.

> How about: every round randomly determine [50% chance per monster]
> which ones will attack the ring wearer and which will be conflicted.
> Or, just to make the code more complex, the chance to be conflicted
> could be influenced by level, monster and player alignment, and
> special features of the monster - mindless ones might be easy to
> influence and intelligent ones, especially the wizard, might have only
> a small chance of being forced to obey the ring.

One thing you _really_ don't want to add in is extra calls to the
random number generator; those things are _expensive_, and they slow the
game down a lot.  If you've paid attention to how the counter works for
long rests, you notice it pauses every seven turns.  That's because the
game only stops to check for random monster generation every seven
turns, and it does a _lot_ of checking when it stops (perhaps every
empty square?)  The reason it does that is that otherwise the game
would be that slow _every_ turn.

The advantage of the checkerboard algorithm is that it only needs 
a couple of XORs and a mask to decide if the monster is in conflict
or attacking the player, something like (all bitwise operations):

    conflict = (row XOR column XOR turn) AND 0x0001.

On a guess, the monsters not in conflict might be cheaper to run,
since they're focused on the player rather than seeking something to
attack, so a room in conflict under the checkerboard approach might
run faster than one fully in conflict.

